5,5,5,0,0,5,0,4,2,4,0,5,0,5],[5,2,2,4,0,0,0,5,5,5,0,5,5,4,0,5,5,5,5],[0,0,4,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,5,0,5,5,5,5,5,5],[5,0,5,0,0,5,0,4,5,0,0,5,5,5,5,5,0,0,5],[5,5,0,5,5,0,2,2,4,0,0,5,5,5,0,5,0,5,5],[5,5,5,0,5,5,5,4,0,5,5,0,5,5,0,0,5,5,0],[5,5,5,5,0,0,0,5,0,0,5,0,5,0,5,0,5,0,0],[0,5,0,5,5,5,0,0,5,5,5,0,0,5,5,5,5,5,5]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-fable-5-1"],"explanation":"## What the grid is made of\n\nEvery input is a noisy field of grey (5) and black (0) cells, with a few small clusters of yellow (4). The outputs are identical to the inputs except that some grey cells turn red (2), and the fact sheet confirms this: the only transition is `5 -> 2`, and every changed cell is adjacent to yellow.\n\nLooking at where the red appears, each yellow cluster is a *fragment of a plus sign* (a centre plus its four orthogonal neighbours). For example in pair 4 the cluster at (4,5),(5,4),(5,6),(6,5) is a plus missing only its centre, and exactly that centre (5,5) becomes red. In pair 1 the cluster (7,2),(8,1),(8,3) is a plus missing its centre (8,2) and its bottom arm (9,2) — both of those become red. Pair 3's cluster (8,8),(9,7) is a diagonal pair, which fits a plus centred at (8,7) (missing centre, top arm (7,7) and left arm (8,6)) — exactly the three cells that turn red.\n\nSo the rule is: **each yellow fragment is completed into a full 3x3 plus; the missing cells of the plus are painted red.** The missing cells are always grey in the input, never black.\n\n## Fixing the ambiguity\n\nSome fragments (e.g. a lone diagonal pair, or a vertical pair of yellows) could in principle belong to more than one plus. The constraint that resolves it is the black cells: a plus is only valid if none of its five cells is black (0) — all missing cells must be grey. With that constraint, I enumerated every candidate centre within one step of each yellow cell, required the whole plus to be inside the grid, to contain the entire yellow cluster, and to have no black cell. Running this on all four train pairs reproduced every output exactly, and on the test input every one of the 7 yellow clusters gets exactly one valid centre:\n\n- (0,14),(1,13) → centre (0,13), a plus clipped by the top edge (the (−1,13) arm is outside, which is fine since train pair 1 has the same situation at the top border)\n- (1,5),(2,6) → centre (1,6)\n- (3,2),(4,1) → centre (3,1)\n- (5,11),(6,10),(6,11) → centre (6,11)\n- (10,13),(11,12),(11,14),(12,13) → centre (11,13), only the centre is missing\n- (11,2),(12,3),(13,2) → centre (12,2)\n- (14,7),(15,8),(16,7) → centre (15,7)\n\n## Construction\n\nStart from the input canvas. For each yellow cluster (8-connected components of colour 4, in reading order), find its unique valid plus centre and recolour the grey cells of that plus to red; I emit the canvas after each cluster is completed, so each stage is one plus being finished.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    out = g.copy()\n\n    def plus_cells(ey, ex):\n        return [(ey, ex), (ey - 1, ex), (ey + 1, ex), (ey, ex - 1), (ey, ex + 1)]\n\n    comps = components(g == 4, diagonal=True)\n    for comp in comps:\n        cs = set(comp)\n        cands = set()\n        for (cy, cx) in cs:\n            # the plus centre is the yellow cell itself or one step away from it\n            for (ey, ex) in plus_cells(cy, cx):\n                inside = [(y, x) for (y, x) in plus_cells(ey, ex) if 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W]\n                if not cs <= set(inside):\n                    continue                       # plus must contain the whole fragment\n                if any(g[y, x] == 0 for (y, x) in inside):\n                    continue                       # missing arms must be grey, never black\n                cands.add((ey, ex))\n        changed = False\n        for (ey, ex) in sorted(cands):\n            for (y, x) in plus_cells(ey, ex):\n                if 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W and g[y, x] == 5:\n                    out[y, x] = 2\n                    changed = True\n        if changed:\n            emit(out.copy(), f\"Complete the yellow fragment at {sorted(cs)} into a plus centred at {sorted(cands)}: paint its missing grey cells red.\")\n\n    emit(out.copy(), \"All yellow fragments completed into plus signs.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"For each yellow cluster (8-connected components of colour 4, in reading order), find its unique valid plus centre and recolour the grey cells of that plus to red; I emit the canvas after each cluster is completed, so each stage is one plus being finished."}],"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"uster (8-connected components of colour 4, in reading order), find its unique valid plus centre and recolour the grey cells of that plus to red; I emit the canvas after each cluster is completed, so each stage is one plus being finished."}],"repair":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the rule is: **each yellow fragment is completed into a full 3x3 plus; the missing cells of the plus are painted red.** The missing cells are always grey in the input, never black."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"t resolves it is the black cells: a plus is only valid if none of its five cells is black (0) — all missing cells must be grey."}],"order":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"For each yellow cluster (8-connected components of colour 4, in reading order), find its unique valid plus centre and recolour the grey cells of that plus to red; I emit the canvas after each cluster is completed, so each stage is one plus being finished."}],"assembly":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The constraint that resolves it is the black cells: a plus is only valid if none of its five cells is black (0) — all missing cells must be grey."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"With that constraint, I enumerated every candidate centre within one step of each yellow cell, required the whole plus to be inside the grid, to contain the entire yellow cluster, and to have no black cell."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"0dfbea59b14ba80041416fc8d98c14725211475b2cde9f167a2ed96a524ab48e","task_sha256":"383288decbada340b956aa1d41446572808d8796af7810ad7801bd1bfd51fd20","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
